,\n  \\\"issue_title\\\":\\\"$issue_title\\\"\n}\")\n```\n\n**Issue:** The `issue_title` variable is embedded directly into JSON without proper escaping. Issue titles can contain quotes, backslashes, and other special characters that would break JSON parsing.\n\n**Status:** ❌ **NOT ESCAPED** - Variable embedded directly without escaping\n\n## Recommendations\n\n1. **For JSON-safe variable embedding**, use `jq` to construct JSON objects:\n   ```bash\n   jq -n --arg sid \"$session_id\" --arg reason \"$reason\" \\\n     '{\"session_id\":$sid,\"reason\":$reason}'\n   ```\n\n2. **For simple escaping**, use these patterns:\n   ```bash\n   # Escape backslashes for Windows paths\n   safe_path=\"${variable//\\\\/\\\\\\\\}\"\n   \n   # Escape quotes\n   safe_var=\"${variable//\\\"/\\\\\\\"}\"\n   \n   # General JSON escape (using jq)\n   escaped_var=$(jq -Rs <<< \"$variable\")\n   ```\n\n3. **Always validate** that variables don't contain malicious content before embedding them in JSON.\n\n## Summary\n\n- **1 instance properly escaped:** The `transcript_path` in the trace-init call (lines 103, 113)\n- **5 instances unescaped:** Variables embedded directly into JSON without proper escaping\n- **Risk level:** Medium to high depending on the content of the variables (especially `issue_title` and `cwd`/`transcript_path` which can contain arbitrary user input)\n\nThe most critical issues are with the `issue_title` and `cwd`/`transcript_path` variables, as they can contain arbitrary content that includes quotes, backslashes, and other characters that would break JSON parsing or potentially allow injection attacks.","background_tasks":[],"session_crons":[]}
